/api.github.com/users/zoowar/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/zoowar/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/zoowar/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/zoowar/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/zoowar/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"created_at":"2015-05-18T17:45:15Z","updated_at":"2015-05-18T17:45:15Z","body":"@normanmaurer When there is no exceptionCaught implemented by any of the pipeline handlers, the framework handles the exception by logging the following message:\n\n> i.n.channel.DefaultChannelPipeline - An exceptionCaught() event was fired, and it reached at the tail of the pipeline. It usually means the last handler in the pipeline did not handle the exception.\n\nIt seems to me that ChannelInitializer/channelRegistered could log the specialized message \"\"Failed to initialize a channel.\" and throw the exception, which will be handled by the default handler or the user defined handler.\n\nI'm not a java programmer, so the only question in my mind is how does the context get closed when an exception occurs in ChannelInitializer? Does it get closed in the default exceptionCaught path? If it doesn't, then ChannelInitializer could override exceptionCaught and close the context. Then document that users who wish to override ChannelInitializer/exceptionCaught must close the context.\n","author_association":"NONE","reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/netty/netty/issues/comments/103146165/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"performed_via_github_app":null,"event":"commented","actor":{"login":"zoowar","id":467917,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjQ2NzkxNw==","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/467917?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/zoowar","html_url":"https://github.com/zoowar","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/user